**Approach: dynamic-programming distance + brute-force ranking.**

```javascript
function editDistance(a, b) {
  const row = Array.from({ length: b.length + 1 }, (_, j) => j);
  for (let i = 1; i <= a.length; i++) {
    let previous = row[0];
    row[0] = i;
    for (let j = 1; j <= b.length; j++) {
      const current = Math.min(row[j] + 1, row[j - 1] + 1, previous + (a[i - 1] === b[j - 1] ? 0 : 1));
      previous = row[j];
      row[j] = current;
    }
  }
  return row[b.length];
}
```

`suggest` normalizes the query, computes `editDistance` to every word in `dictionary.words()`, keeps those within `MAX_DISTANCE`, sorts with one comparator (distance, then frequency descending, then `<` on the word) and returns the first `limit` words.

Keeping one row of the table makes the distance O(|a| · |b|) time and O(|b|) memory. A lookup is O(N · |a| · |b|) for N dictionary words, which is fine for the ~600-word base dictionary.

Things worth checking: the query is normalized but results are dictionary keys; `recieve` ranks `relieve` (distance 1) above the more common `receive` (distance 2), because distance comes first; and ties in frequency are broken alphabetically (`weird` before `wired`).

