**Where phase 2 breaks.** Every lookup runs the DP against all 20,000 words, about a million cell updates, or roughly 20 ms in V8. 1,000 lookups take about 20 s.

**Optimized approach: symmetric-delete index.** If `editDistance(a, b) <= 2`, deleting at most 2 characters from each produces a common string. Index those deletion variants once, then verify only the words that share a variant with the query:

```javascript
function deletes(word, depth = MAX_DISTANCE) {
  const variants = new Set([word]);
  let frontier = [word];
  for (let level = 0; level < depth; level++) {
    const next = [];
    for (const w of frontier) {
      for (let i = 0; i < w.length; i++) {
        const shorter = w.slice(0, i) + w.slice(i + 1);
        if (!variants.has(shorter)) { variants.add(shorter); next.push(shorter); }
      }
    }
    frontier = next;
  }
  return variants;
}
// constructor: for each entry, for each variant of deletes(entry): index[variant].push(entry)
// suggest: union index[v] for v of deletes(key), verify with editDistance <= 2, sort as before
```

The candidate set is a **superset** of the true answers (sharing a variant does not prove distance ≤ 2), so verification with the real distance is required. The ranking is unchanged, so results match phase 2 exactly.

**Complexity.** Build: O(N · L²) variants, about 660k keys for 20,000 words (around half a second). Lookup: O(L²) variants plus a DP for each of a few dozen candidates, under 0.1 ms, so more than 200× faster than phase 2.

**Tradeoffs.** Memory grows with N · L², and the index is a snapshot, so words added later need incremental updates. A trie walked with one DP row per node uses far less memory and also passes, but its lookups are about 10× slower. A BK-tree or length buckets with an early-exit DP still visit a large fraction of the dictionary for distance 2. Generating every edit of the query (Norvig-style) produces about 200k strings at distance 2 and needs the dictionary's alphabet, including `'` and accents.
